; The same two questions, answered a second time by a different part of `writ`.
;
; This file is not a replacement for the claims file. `modality-cross-check.sh`
; runs both and compares them; the two share no code, so a disagreement is a
; bug in one of them rather than a number to adjust.
;
; POLARITY, which is easy to get backwards:
;   `possible F` is the SATISFYING set — non-empty means the property holds.
;   `live F` cannot be said positively, so it is the COUNTEREXAMPLE set: the
;   situations from which F can no longer be reached. EMPTY means it holds.
(load "ct.rules")

; `settled` is spelled with two literals here rather than `(is prod.old prod.new)`,
; because a rules guard compares a chain against a literal and not against a
; second chain. It is the same set: the goal already pins `new` to r2.
(satisfies completes
  (and (is flag-col.state absent) (is prod.old r2) (is prod.new r2)))

(can-reach can-still-finish completes)
(trapped no-dead-ends can-still-finish)
